":"","poi_name":null,"subject":null,"author":"","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/889017","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/889017"}},{"id":"889011","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"891661","description":"Marshall Ezelle with clients on the Lowe","caption":"","poi_name":null,"subject":null,"author":"","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/889011","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/889011"}},{"id":"889005","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"891655","description":"Brian O'Neil takes some clients down the canyon","caption":"","poi_name":null,"subject":null,"author":"","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/889005","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/889005"}}],"detail":{"id":"32","permitUrl":"","permitInfo":"","description":"**Put-in:**\n\nAbove Keystone canyon on the Richardson highway, 16 miles from Valdez.\n\n**Take-out:**\n\nThere is a turnoff at the end of the canyon that leads to a gravel bar. This large gravel bar connects to a road that is upstream of the Alyeska Pipeline access bridge. The gravel bar is in a constant state of flux so check with locals or call Pangea Adventures in Valdez for accessibility.\n\n**Description:**\n\nThe Keystone canyon secton of the Lowe is the staple for the Valdez whitewater community and where many local boaters have first experienced the draw of the river.\n\nCommerically rafted by Pangaea Adventures in Valdez, the roadside run is relatively straight-forward continous whitewater consisting of big wave trains, sticky hydualics, boils and other compression features that will hone the skills of aspiring paddlers. The canyon itself is goregous, where giant cascades flow down the steep walls and the Richardson highway above the river drifts into a distant memory.\n\nFun rapids with names like Toliet Bowl, 14 mile, and Three Bears offer consistent action. A major highlight is Bridalveil Falls, a 600 foot cataract where one can stand in it's pounding spray.\n\nShuttle is short, making for multiple runs in a day, and can be done by jogging, bicylcing, or hitching.","averageGradient":"40","maxGradient":"50","length":"5","correlations":[],"pointOfInterestIDs":["108378","113614"],"geometry":null,"bannerPhotoID":"0","editedAt":"2018-03-19T06:00:01.000Z"},"updatedAt":1778556479},"meta":{"values":{"pointOfInterests.0.location.latitude":[["custom","decimal.js"]],"pointOfInterests.0.location.longitude":[["custom","decimal.js"]],"pointOfInterests.0.distance":[["custom","decimal.js"]],"pointOfInterests.1.location.latitude":[["custom","decimal.js"]],"pointOfInterests.1.location.longitude":[["custom","decimal.js"]],"pointOfInterests.1.distance":[["custom","decimal.js"]],"detail.averageGradient":[["custom","decimal.js"]],"detail.maxGradient":[["custom","decimal.js"]],"detail.length":[["custom","decimal.js"]],"detail.editedAt":["Date"]}}}}}]